---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../../../components/Hero.astro';
import Cta from '../../../components/Cta.astro';
import SectionNav from '../../../components/SectionNav.astro';
---
<Base title={"When the Hotel Owner Is a Customer and a Neighbour"} description={"How a community bank can handle a problem hotel loan when the borrower is a long-standing customer, and when a loan sale is not the right answer."}>
  <Hero eyebrow="For community banks" title={"When the owner is a customer and a neighbour."} lead={"In a small market the borrower is also a customer, often with deposits, other credit, and a guarantor the bank knows. How to handle that, and when a sale is not the answer."} />
  <SectionNav section="communityBanks" />
  <main id="main">
<section class="section"><div class="wrap intro">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">The relationship</p><h2>The owner should not learn about it from someone else.</h2>
        <div class="body">
          <p>The most common worry about selling a loan in a community is the borrower: a customer of long standing, a guarantor who lives nearby, a family that runs the hotel.</p>
          <p>The practical answer is that a loan sale changes who holds the loan, not what the loan says. The note, the mortgage, and any guaranty stay the same. What changes is who makes the decisions, and a holder that bought the loan at a different basis often has room to agree to terms the original lender could not.</p>
        </div></div>
      <dl class="pillars">
        <div><dt>Tell the borrower first</dt><dd>Whatever the bank decides, the owner is better off hearing it from the bank than from a letter. Take the advice of the bank's counsel on what to say and when.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>We do not go around the bank</dt><dd>We do not contact the borrower, guarantors, the brand, or hotel staff without the bank's consent. After closing we contact the borrower directly, with notice of the transfer and where payments go.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>The documents do not change</dt><dd>The borrower's obligations and rights are the ones already in the note, mortgage, and any guaranty.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>We prefer agreement</dt><dd>Negotiated outcomes are faster and cost both sides less. Where no agreement can be reached, we rely on the rights in the loan documents.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section alt"><div class="wrap">
      <div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">Sometimes a sale is not the answer</p><h2>If the owner can find the money, a discounted payoff may be better.</h2>
        <p>A discounted payoff or a deed in lieu with the borrower can beat a loan sale, because the bank keeps the relationship and ends the loan at a known figure. The question to ask is whether the owner can actually close. If the owner has a source of funds and a date, that route deserves a fair chance. If not, a loan sale gives the bank a date it controls.</p></div>
      <div class="cells">
        <div><h3>Discounted payoff</h3><p>The bank accepts less than the full balance in exchange for payment now. It needs someone with the money, and a date.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Deed in lieu</h3><p>The owner hands over the hotel by agreement, often in exchange for a release from the guaranty. The bank then holds an OREO hotel.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Loan sale</h3><p>The bank is paid at closing and the loan, the file, and the workout transfer. We can then work with the owner on new terms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">General information, not legal advice. Communications with a borrower in a workout, and any claims the borrower may make, are matters for the bank's counsel (see <a href="/glossary/#lender-liability">lender liability</a>).</p>
    </div></section>

    <section class="section alt"><div class="wrap"><div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">Related reading</p></div><ul class="toc"><li><a href="/borrowers/">For hotel owners: what changes when a loan is sold <span>Hotel owners</span></a></li><li><a href="/blog/when-your-hotel-loan-is-sold/">What changes, and what does not, when your hotel loan is sold <span>Blog</span></a></li><li><a href="/blog/discounted-payoffs-on-hotel-loans/">Discounted payoffs on hotel loans: what a lender should weigh <span>Blog</span></a></li><li><a href="/glossary/#deed-in-lieu">Deed in lieu of foreclosure <span>Glossary</span></a></li></ul></div></section>
  </main>
  <Cta title={"Discuss a hotel loan in confidence."} text={"A conversation and an indicative price carry no obligation to sell."} label={"Start a conversation"} />
</Base>
